import org.elasticsearch.common.logging.LogEvent;
import java.lang.StringBuilder;

// Assume you already have a LogEvent instance named `logEvent`
LogEvent logEvent = /* … */;

// Create the StringBuilder that will hold the formatted log line
StringBuilder logBuilder = new StringBuilder();

// Append the node‑ID (if available) to the StringBuilder
functions.NodeIdConverter_format(
        new functions.NodeIdConverter_format.Params(
                /* event */ logEvent,
                /* toAppendTo */ logBuilder
        )
);

// Now `logBuilder` contains the original log text plus the node‑ID
System.out.println(logBuilder.toString());
```

### What the helper does

* **`event`** – The `LogEvent` that carries the logging information.  
* **`toAppendTo`** – The `StringBuilder` that will receive the node‑ID.  
* The helper checks whether the node‑ID is present in the `LogEvent`.  
  * If it is, it appends the node‑ID to the supplied `StringBuilder`.  
  * If it isn’t, the `StringBuilder` remains unchanged.

### Quick sanity check

If you want to verify that the node‑ID was actually appended, you can add a simple check:

```java
if (logBuilder.indexOf("nodeId=") >= 0) {
    System.out.println("Node ID successfully appended.");
} else {
    System.out.println("No node ID found in the LogEvent.");
}
```

That’s all you need—just call the helper with your `LogEvent` and `StringBuilder`, and it will take care of the rest.

